    {
      "category": "Default",
      "hideRanking": false,
      "color": "#ffe500",
      "description": "This year's Fortune Global 500 generated $33.3 trillion in revenues and $2.1 trillion in profits.",
      "permalink": "https://fortune.com/ranking/global500/2020/",
      "rank": 317,
      "slug": "global500",
      "title": "Fortune Global 500",
      "year": 2020,
      "image": {
        "src": "https://fortune.com/img-assets/wp-content/uploads/2020/08/G500-Featured-Image-Logo-2020.jpg",
        "title": "G500-Featured Image-Logo-2020",
        "alt": "G500-Featured Image-Logo-2020",
        "caption": "",
        "credit": "Sergi Delgado",
        "__typename": "rankingListItemImage"
      },
      "itemDescription": "Revenues at General Dynamics were up 8.7% in 2019, compared with the previous year, to $39.4 billion; profits, though, lagged compared with some competitors', growing just 4.2%. One big win was in tanks and weapons systems, where sales were up 12.3% for the year. That uptick came despite a diplomatic imbroglio between Canada\u2019s government and Saudi Arabia that delayed payment on part of a $13 billion contract to sell armored vehicles to Saudi Arabia. (The dispute was resolved in April 2020.) But GD\u2019s information technology segment stalled, posting a mere 1.9% growth. So far, 2020 is off to a mixed start: Coronavirus-related travel restrictions and production problems hammered GD\u2019s first-quarter aerospace revenues, especially the core Gulfstream business jet line, while IT sales slumped even further.",
      "uri": "/ranking/global500/2020/",
      "__typename": "_ItemRankingListsType"
    },
    {
      "category": "Default",
      "hideRanking": false,
      "color": "#000000",
      "description": "This year's Fortune 500 marks the 66th running of the list.",
      "permalink": "https://fortune.com/ranking/fortune500/2020/",
      "rank": 83,
      "slug": "fortune500",
      "title": "Fortune 500",
      "year": 2020,
      "image": {
        "src": "https://fortune.com/img-assets/wp-content/uploads/2020/05/F500-2020-083-General-Dynamics.jpg",
        "title": "F500-2020-083-General-Dynamics",
        "alt": "F500-2020-083-General-Dynamics",
        "caption": "A platoon of M1 Abrahams battle tanks, manufactured by General Dynamics Land Systems, sit on the dockside following delivery during the U.S.-led NATO Defender Europe-20 Exercise at the Port of Bremerhaven in Bremerhaven, Germany, on Friday, Feb. 21, 2020. President Trump and French President Emmanuel Macron discussed the importance of NATO, French and U.S. counterterrorism efforts in the Sahel region of Africa, and other bilateral issues, in a call Tuesday, White House spokesman Judd Deere says in Twitter post. Photographer: Krisztian Bocsi/Bloomberg via Getty Images",
        "credit": "Krisztian BocsiBloomberg/Getty Images",
        "__typename": "rankingListItemImage"
      },
      "itemDescription": "General Dynamics climbed nine places on the <em>Fortune</em> 500 this year, quite an accomplishment for a company that\u2019s been on the list since its inception. Revenues were up 8.7%, year-over-year, to $39.4 billion; profits, though, lagged some competitors', growing just 4.2% for the year. One big win was in tanks and weapons systems, where sales were up 12.3% for the year. That uptick came despite a diplomatic imbroglio between Canada\u2019s government and Saudi Arabia that delayed payment on part of a $13 billion contract to sell armored vehicles to Saudi Arabia. (The dispute was resolved in April 2020.) But GD\u2019s information technology segment stalled out at just 1.9% growth. So far, 2020 is off to a mixed start: Coronavirus-related travel restrictions and production problems hammered GD\u2019s first-quarter aerospace revenues, especially the core Gulfstream business jet line, while IT sales slumped even further.",
      "uri": "/ranking/fortune500/2020/",
      "__typename": "_ItemRankingListsType"
    },
